    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Stage 2 hypertension, confirmed (I10)</content>. Home readings confirm stage 2 hypertension. No albuminuria, normal kidney function, ECG voltage for left ventricular hypertrophy, and a 10-year cardiovascular risk of about 14%. The 2025 guideline advises two first-line medicines of different classes at this level; for a Black woman a calcium channel blocker and a thiazide-type diuretic are effective choices. No single tablet combines these two, so two tablets. Dr. Osei-Hargreaves agrees, and advises watching sodium and standing blood pressure closely, since she is 71 and lives alone. [S]<list><item>Amlodipine 5 mg and chlorthalidone 12.5 mg each morning.</item><item>Sodium, potassium and creatinine in about four weeks.</item><item>Sick-day rule: stop chlorthalidone and call if she cannot eat or drink normally.</item><item>Echocardiogram booked.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Fall risk (Z91.81)</content>. STEADI: Timed Up and Go 15 seconds, a below-average chair stand and poor tandem balance, with fear of falling; no orthostatic drop today. [S]<list><item>Physical therapy for strength and balance, with a cane.</item><item>Occupational therapy home visit for the stairs and bathroom.</item><item>Firm, closed shoes.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Knee osteoarthritis (M17.0)</content>. Pain 6 out of 10; KOOS JR 52.5 last week. [S]<list><item>Diclofenac gel; exercise therapy with the physical therapist.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure in the clinic (seated, large cuff, two readings) and at home; target below 130/80 mmHg</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>176/98 mmHg in the clinic; home seven-day average 168/96, confirming the diagnosis</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Orthostatic blood pressure (lying and standing)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No orthostatic drop before treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and BMI (5% weight-loss goal for knee osteoarthritis)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>78.0 kg, BMI 31.6</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Cardiac auscultation and edema</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No murmur, gallop or edema</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sodium, potassium, creatinine and eGFR</td><td>Two to four weeks after starting or changing a diuretic or ARB; then every six months on these medicines</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Normal at the first visit; due about four weeks after starting chlorthalidone</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipid panel</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>LDL 146 mg/dL at the first visit</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio, hemoglobin A1c and TSH</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>UACR 18 mg/g, A1c 5.6% and TSH 2.1 at the first visit</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>12-lead ECG</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Left ventricular hypertrophy by voltage at the first visit</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Echocardiogram</td><td>At diagnosis if stage 2 or left ventricular hypertrophy</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Booked for the four-week review</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Fundoscopic examination</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Arteriolar narrowing without hemorrhage or papilledema at the first visit</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Ankle-brachial index</td><td>Every two to three years at 65 or older</td><td>Completed today</td><td>1.04 right, 1.08 left: normal</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>10-year cardiovascular risk</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About 14% over ten years; statin decision at the four-week review with her</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 6, mild, tied to loneliness and fear of falling; item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Knee pain score (0 to 10)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>6 out of 10</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Knee function (KOOS JR)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Up to date</td><td>KOOS JR 52.5 a week ago; next at the four-week review</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Knee examination (range of motion, effusion, alignment, crepitus)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Crepitus and bony enlargement, no effusion</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exercise therapy, tai chi and self-management</td><td>Ongoing</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Physical therapy for strength, balance and the knees, with a cane</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>NSAID safety (topical diclofenac; no oral NSAIDs)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Diclofenac gel only; no oral NSAIDs</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>STEADI assessment: Timed Up and Go, 30-second chair stand, 4-stage balance</td><td>At diagnosis of fall risk, then yearly or with a fall</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Timed Up and Go 15 seconds; 30-second chair stand 7; tandem stance under 10 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medicine review for fall risk and interactions (Beers criteria)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No sedatives or anticholinergic medicines; chlorthalidone flagged for sodium monitoring</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Home safety, footwear, vision and assistive device</td><td>At diagnosis of fall risk, then yearly</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Occupational therapy home visit booked; closed shoes advised; reading glasses adequate</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Her own remedies: interactions and how she uses them</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Liquorice paused by h